19. SECURITY AND DATA PROTECTION

Security must be implemented at the backend level.

Required protections:
	•	Secure authentication.
	•	Password hashing through the authentication provider.
	•	Protected routes.
	•	Backend authorization.
	•	Database access policies.
	•	Row Level Security where supported.
	•	Secure session management.
	•	Input validation.
	•	Protection against unauthorized access.
	•	Protection against privilege escalation.
	•	Secure file uploads.
	•	Appropriate error handling.

Role security

The backend must verify the user’s role before allowing administrative actions.

Students must never be able to:
	•	Change their own role.
	•	Approve their own account.
	•	Change their account status.
	•	Access another student’s private data.
	•	Access administrative endpoints.

Administrators must never be able to:
	•	Assign themselves Super Admin privileges.
	•	Modify the Super Admin role.
	•	Override Super Admin permissions.
	•	Access functionality they have not been authorized to use.

The Super Admin must retain full control.

Never expose:
	•	Passwords.
	•	Secret API keys.
	•	Private service credentials.
	•	Database service-role keys.
	•	Authentication secrets.

Do not place sensitive credentials in frontend code.

Use environment variables and secure backend configuration.

